FDMR's change to DSM2 v8.5.1's General Transport Model (GTM, ref:dsm2), for suspended
sediment in the Delta (issue #16). Applied by build_dsm2.sh before building.

GTM as released takes cohesive sediment's settling velocity from a grain size
(Stokes/van Rijn, ref:vanrijn1984) and its critical shear stress from Brownlie's
Shields curve (ref:brownlie1981),
deposits only below that same stress, and needs DWR's sediment bed module
(its zone files, which DWR's public inputs do not include). Without that
module the sediment code reads an unset bed and requires a temperature input.

This patch adds four GTM scalars and makes the module run without the bed:

  sed_settling        settling velocity of every class, m/s
  sed_tau_erosion     critical bed shear stress for erosion, Pa
  sed_deposit_always  deposition = ws * c at any bed shear stress
  sed_bed_initial     settled sediment on the bed at the start, kg/m2

Without the bed module the bed holds only what has settled on it (kg per m2 of
bed, per class), updated with Heun's two stages as the water is, and erosion
(Partheniades: M (tau_b / tau_ce - 1), ref:partheniades1965) takes no more than is there. This is
the set-up of Achete, van der Wegen, Roelvink and Jaffe (2015, ref:achete2015),
Hydrol. Earth Syst. Sci. 19, 2837-2857, doi:10.5194/hess-19-2837-2015,
sections 2.1 and 3.4: one mud fraction, D = ws c, no bed sediment at the start. Unset, every
scalar leaves GTM as DWR released it.

It also writes, each HDF5 output step, every cell's settled sediment (kg/m2)
and erosion flux (kg/m2/s) to <output>_bed.bin, for pollutants that ride on
the sediment (issue #16; fdmr_plugins.dsm2.fields reads it).

